    {
      "slug": "reddit-movers-video-proof-claim",
      "script": "They lost WHAT? My cross-country movers arrived, and the antique clock I’d wrapped myself was gone from the truck. Their contract required full-value claims to include proof from before loading, so I opened the eleven-minute walkthrough I filmed that morning: every box timestamped, narrated, the clock at the four-minute mark. Their adjuster offered sixty dollars—the depreciated released-value rate. I sent the video back with their own certificate-of-insurance clause. Legal got it within the hour. Two weeks later: a check for the clock’s full appraised value, plus an apology email. Funny how readable a mover’s contract becomes when you have the receipts. Does that sixty-dollar rate survive an eleven-minute video?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up the four-word hook, moved the proof and lowball offer forward, kept every number, entity, and claim step, then landed the kicker on the eleven-minute video."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-subcontractor-lien-payback",
      "script": "He thought I’d what? I finished the electrical rough-in on a twelve-unit building for a general contractor who’d stiffed me twice before. After final walkthrough, he ghosted for six weeks—forty-one thousand dollars unpaid. I didn’t call again. I filed a mechanic’s lien within my state’s deadline and mailed notice to him and the property owner. Bad timing for him: the owner was refinancing that month, and the title company would not close with an active lien on the report. The owner demanded it be fixed. The contractor, suddenly allergic to missing paperwork, wired the full amount plus the late fee within two days. Title cleared, I released the lien, refinance closed on schedule. Do unpaid invoices still “disappear” when a lien is on the report?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up the question hook, front-loaded the unpaid amount, tightened the refinancing pressure, kept every fact and shot beat, and ended on the active lien."
    }
  ],
